{
  "teamId": 114,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Cleveland Guardians were a team that knew how to win without making it look pretty. From the opening weeks in April, they absorbed early body blows — a Zach Cozart shot off McAllister, a Miguel Sano bomb off Belisle — but answered with clutch moments of their own, like Yan Gomes' two-run blast and Edwin Encarnación's surging power. The team stumbled to a .500-ish pace through April and May, weathering blown leads and bullpen hiccups, but scattered heroes kept them afloat. Michael Brantley delivered a grand slam, Greg Allen hit his first career home run in a wild comeback, and the offense showed enough pop to stay dangerous even when the pitching wavered.\n\nJune and early July brought continued competitive baseball, though the bullpen — Cody Allen in particular — provided more than a few nightmarish moments. Allen surrendered crucial long balls to Jeimer Candelario and Joey Gallo, and Dan Otero's catastrophic outing against Joey Votto in July stood as the season's single most damaging play. Yet the Guardians kept grinding, their 91-win total built on sheer accumulation of effort rather than any one dominant stretch.\n\nAugust was the turning point. The Guardians went 19-9, their best month of the season, and it showed in the balance of momentum. Yonder Alonso delivered a huge two-run blast, Francisco Lindor kept producing, and even when Cody Allen had another rough night in Kansas City, the team had enough cushion to absorb the damage. The division was theirs to lose, and they didn't lose it. September brought a regression to .500 and some alarming late-season moments — Brad Hand's gut-punch home run allowed to Ji-Man Choi in the season's most catastrophic single play cost them nearly a full win in win probability — but Jason Kipnis' September grand slam reminded everyone this lineup had bite.\n\nThe postseason brought Cleveland face-to-face with the Houston Astros, and the defending champions proved too much. Corey Kluber, the ace, couldn't keep Alex Bregman in the park in Game 1. Francisco Lindor answered twice with home runs across Games 2 and 3, providing flashes of hope that the Guardians could hang, but Andrew Miller's struggles and Marwin Gonzalez's back-breaking doubles in the late innings of Game 2 extinguished the rallies. By Game 3, George Springer was punishing Cleveland starters again, and another Gonzalez double finished the job. The Astros swept, and a strong 91-win season ended in the cold silence of October elimination.",
